I haven't been given HyperFrames' API, so this is a framework-agnostic spec with a small sketch. Map the `render(t)` function onto however HyperFrames exposes seekable time.

## Core rule for seekability

Every visual property is a pure function of the scene time `t`. Nothing is driven by wall-clock timers, CSS animations that run on their own, or state carried over from the previous frame. Jumping to any `t` then gives the same result as playing up to it.

## Timeline (1.4s total)

| Element | Start | Duration | Motion |
|---|---|---|---|
| Heading, word by word | 0.00s + 0.08s per word | 0.60s | Each word slides up from `translateY(100%)` to 0 inside an `overflow: hidden` line mask. Opacity goes 0 to 1 over the first 40% of the word's duration. |
| Card 1, 2, 3 | 0.45s + 0.12s per card | 0.55s | `translateY(32px)` to 0, `scale(0.96)` to 1, opacity 0 to 1. |

Easing is easeOutCubic for everything: `1 - (1 - p)^3`.

## Sketch

```js
const clamp01 = x => Math.min(1, Math.max(0, x));
const easeOut = p => 1 - Math.pow(1 - p, 3);
const prog = (t, start, dur) => easeOut(clamp01((t - start) / dur));

function render(t) {
  words.forEach((el, i) => {
    const p = prog(t, i * 0.08, 0.6);
    el.style.transform = `translateY(${(1 - p) * 100}%)`;
    el.style.opacity = clamp01(p / 0.4);
  });

  cards.forEach((el, i) => {
    const p = prog(t, 0.45 + i * 0.12, 0.55);
    el.style.transform = `translateY(${(1 - p) * 32}px) scale(${0.96 + 0.04 * p})`;
    el.style.opacity = p;
  });
}
```

## Why this supports jumping to any time

- **Clamped progress:** at `t < start` an element sits at its initial state, and at `t > start + dur` it sits at its final state. Scrubbing backward or past the end is safe.
- **Idempotent:** `render(t)` sets every property explicitly on every call, so there is no dependence on the previous frame.
- **Index-based stagger:** delays come from the element's index, not from a sequence of callbacks.
- **No randomness:** if you add jitter or variation, derive it from a seeded value per element, never `Math.random()` at render time.
- **Initial state before first render:** call `render(0)` on mount so nothing flashes in its final position.

If HyperFrames provides its own timeline or tween primitive, express the same offsets and durations there and make sure it is paused and driven by the scene's seek time rather than running freely.

