I don't have HyperFrames API details in the facts supplied, so this is written as a pure function of time. You can map it onto whatever timeline or seek hook the framework provides.

## Seekability principle

Every visual property is computed directly from the scene time `t` (seconds). There is no accumulated state, no delta-time stepping, no `Date.now()`, no event-triggered tweens, and no randomness without a fixed seed. Jumping to any `t`, forward or backward, then renders exactly the same frame as playing through to it.

## Timing (total about 1.1 s)

| Element | Start | Duration | Motion | Easing |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Heading, word *i* | `0.00 + i·0.06` | 0.50 | Slides up from `translateY(100%)` to `0` inside an `overflow:hidden` mask, with opacity 0→1 over the first 40% | easeOutCubic |
| Heading underline or accent (optional) | 0.40 | 0.40 | `scaleX` 0→1, origin left | easeInOutCubic |
| Card | 0.35 | 0.55 | `translateY` 28px→0, `scale` 0.96→1, opacity 0→1 | easeOutCubic |
| Card contents (optional) | 0.55 + i·0.08 | 0.35 | Fade and rise 8px | easeOutQuad |

The card begins while the heading is still finishing, so the two read as one gesture.

## Sketch

```js
const clamp01 = x => Math.min(1, Math.max(0, x));
const prog = (t, start, dur) => clamp01((t - start) / dur);
const easeOutCubic = p => 1 - Math.pow(1 - p, 3);

function render(t) {
  // Heading
  words.forEach((el, i) => {
    const p = easeOutCubic(prog(t, i * 0.06, 0.5));
    el.style.transform = `translateY(${(1 - p) * 100}%)`;
    el.style.opacity = clamp01(p / 0.4);
  });

  // Card
  const c = easeOutCubic(prog(t, 0.35, 0.55));
  card.style.transform = `translateY(${(1 - c) * 28}px) scale(${0.96 + 0.04 * c})`;
  card.style.opacity = c;
}
```

`render(t)` is called on every seek and every playback tick. Because progress is clamped, `t < start` gives the hidden initial state and `t > start + dur` gives the final state, so scrubbing past either end is safe.

## Notes for the preview

- Set initial hidden styles through `render(0)`, not CSS-only defaults, so a seek to 0 is identical to first load.
- Split the heading into word spans up front, not during animation, so layout is stable at every `t`.
- If the framework provides its own timeline object, register these as tweens on it rather than running a separate clock. Two clocks will drift out of sync when you seek.
- If you use `will-change: transform, opacity` on the card, remove it after the animation ends to avoid holding a compositor layer.

